  "prompt_text": "The figure is a simple schematic showing two routes for making solid host–guest complexes. On the left, the components are introduced separately: orange hexagon-like shapes represent the host and small teal circles represent the guest. In one route, both are mixed in a liquid in a beaker, and co-crystallization or precipitation produces a solid where the guest appears incorporated within individual host units. This top pathway suggests discrete inclusion complexes forming directly from solution.\n\nThe second route starts from a pre-existing solid host phase exposed to guest vapor. Teal guest molecules diffuse from the vapor into the orange host crystal lattice, leading to a reorganized solid-state host–guest structure. In the product on the lower right, the guests seem to occupy spaces between neighboring host units within an extended solid framework rather than isolated molecular complexes.\n\nOverall, the diagram compares solution-based assembly with vapor diffusion into a solid. Its main message is that host–guest materials can be created either by crystallizing both components together from solution or by inserting guest molecules into an already solid host through vapor uptake.",
